    "result_text": "`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests green. The change is implemented and verified.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe create-owner pipeline already had a `RequireUniqueHousehold` step that skips its duplicate check when `sharesHousehold=true` (allowing an owner at an address already used by a same-lastName owner). What was missing was the **`householdId`** \u2014 a stable shared identifier assigned to owners in the same household, and returned on the response. I added that:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `householdId` string field to the `Owner` response schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` gains `getHouseholdId()`/`setHouseholdId()`).\n\n2. **`Household.java`** (new, in `rest/function/owner/`) \u2014 a small utility with:\n   - `normalize(...)` \u2014 trim, collapse whitespace, lower-case (the household-identity comparison).\n   - `id(lastName, address)` \u2014 a stable identifier derived from the normalized last name + address (first 8 bytes of SHA-256, upper-hex). Because it's a pure function of the household fields, every owner in the same household deterministically gets the **same** value, with no stored column or lookup needed.\n\n3. **`OwnerMapper`** \u2014 mapped `householdId` in `toOwnerDto` via `Household.id(owner.getLastName(), owner.getAddress())`, so both `POST /api/owners` (201 body) and `GET /api/owners/{id}` expose it.\n\n4. **`RequireUniqueHousehold`** \u2014 refactored to reuse `Household.normalize`, keeping the duplicate-detection notion of \"same household\" identical to what the identifier is derived from.\n\n## Result\n\nThe acceptance test `coreJoinersShareHouseholdId` \u2014 which creates owner A, then B and C sharing A's last name and address with `sharesHousehold=true`, and asserts B and C return an identical, non-blank `householdId` \u2014 passes. Full suite: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`, `BUILD SUCCESS`.\n\nOne design note: because `householdId` is *derived* rather than stored, owner A (created without `sharesHousehold`) also returns the same `householdId` as B and C automatically \u2014 so \"both get the same identifier\" holds for every member of the household, not just the joiners.",
